{
  "plan": {
    "operation": "change_assumption",
    "parent_shortcut": "fixed_environment",
    "response_change": {
      "type": "interaction_emergence",
      "parent_relation": "The target mapping depends on q and i with temperature only through a self-heated resistor term R*(1+tanh(alpha_T*T_res)); the capacitor branch is assumed temperature-invariant, so heating changes only damping.",
      "child_relation": "Allow capacitor capacitance to vary with resistor temperature rise, so the restoring term q/C becomes a coupled function of q and T_res as well as the damping term depending on i and T_res.",
      "affected_inputs": [
        "q",
        "i",
        "T_res",
        "C"
      ],
      "observable_effect": "Across otherwise identical discharges, larger self-heating causes not only stronger damping but also a shifted oscillation timescale/restoring slope, producing a different q-to-di_dt relation at matched i and T_res levels than the parent's damping-only modification."
    },
    "diagnostic_groups": [
      {
        "name": "restoring_ratio",
        "expression": "q/C",
        "role": "baseline capacitor restoring contribution to current acceleration"
      },
      {
        "name": "thermal_damping",
        "expression": "R*(1 + tanh(alpha_T*T_res))*i",
        "role": "existing temperature-dependent resistive voltage drop"
      },
      {
        "name": "heating_level",
        "expression": "alpha_T*T_res",
        "role": "dimensionless self-heating scale governing when thermal property curvature becomes visible"
      }
    ],
    "activation_condition": "Observable when self-heating is large enough that resistor temperature rise is not negligible and the capacitor's effective capacitance has measurable thermal drift over the same excursion; this is most visible during moderate-to-strong current episodes before oscillations fully decay.",
    "train_hidden_contrast": "In training, mild heating mainly shows damping growth; in holdout with larger but still admissible heating, the same thermal excursion also shifts the restoring branch, changing oscillation timing and the q-conditioned slope of di_dt beyond what a single damping-only law can match.",
    "continuation_path": "Recover the parent when the capacitance thermal coefficient tends to zero or when T_res remains near zero throughout the observed trajectory.",
    "freedom_change": "none",
    "promoted_symbol": null,
    "promotion_reason": "",
    "gap_statement": "The current model treats self-heating as affecting only resistor dissipation, while keeping the capacitor's constitutive relation strictly temperature-invariant during the same thermal excursion.",
    "baseline_assumption": "Capacitance is constant and independent of temperature during discharge.",
    "scientific_mechanism": "Dielectric properties in real capacitors can drift with temperature, so resistor self-heating shared by the local component environment can change the effective capacitance and therefore the instantaneous restoring voltage-charge relation.",
    "affected_symbols": [
      "q",
      "i",
      "T_res",
      "C"
    ],
    "rationale": "A realistic next refinement is to relax the parent's constant-capacitance assumption rather than adding a wholly new process. The parent target mapping has a fixed-environment shortcut: temperature modifies only the resistive branch, so at fixed q and T_res the q contribution to di_dt is always exactly q/(L*C), independent of heating history except through the separately supplied damping term. The child would couple T_res into the capacitor branch too, creating a genuine target-mapping change because the scored di_dt would then vary with T_res even at the same q and i in a way not reducible to damping alone. The concrete distinguishing condition is to compare portions of trajectories with similar q and i magnitudes but different self-heating levels T_res, while holding C, L, R, alpha_T, C_th, and tau_th fixed across the run. The parent can only change the coefficient multiplying i under heating; it cannot simultaneously alter the restoring q slope with one shared parameter set. This effect is scientifically admissible when thermal drift of capacitance is non-negligible over the resistor-heating range experienced in the benchmark; if the capacitor is effectively temperature-stable or thermally isolated, the refinement may be weak, and the parent is then recovered."
  },
